use ../lib/CodingRule

from ./CertC import CertC

export coding-rule CertCFio34C:
    standard: @{CertC}
    ruleId: FIO34-C
    title:
        Distinguish between characters read from a file and EOF or WEOF
    statement:
        Keep the result of fgetc, getc, getchar and their wide forms in an
        int or wint_t, never a char. When the result equals EOF or WEOF,
        call feof and ferror to find out whether the stream really ended or
        failed.
    rationale:
        Narrowing the result to char can turn a valid byte into EOF or make
        EOF unreachable, and on platforms where char and int have the same
        width a real character can compare equal to EOF. CERT rates
        likelihood probable, detectable yes, repairable yes, priority P18,
        level L1.
    exceptions:
        Functions such as fclose, fflush, fputs and scanf return EOF only as
        a status code, so their results may be compared with EOF directly.
    severity: high
    noncompliant:
        ```c
        \\\
        size_t count_lines(FILE *in) {
            size_t lines = 0;
            char c;
            while ((c = getc(in)) != EOF) {
                if (c == '\n') {
                    lines++;
                }
            }
            return lines;
        }
        \\\
        ```
    compliant:
        ```c
        \\\
        int count_lines(FILE *in, size_t *lines) {
            int c;
            *lines = 0;
            while ((c = getc(in)) != EOF) {
                if (c == '\n') {
                    (*lines)++;
                }
            }
            return ferror(in) ? -1 : 0;
        }
        \\\
        ```
    languages: [c]
    tags: [io, error-handling]
    source: https://cmu-sei.github.io/secure-coding-standards/sei-cert-c-coding-standard/rules/input-output-fio/fio34-c
